---
// MOVE-OUT CLEANING — `/move-out-cleaning-san-jose/`. Rebuilt 2026-09-01 as the fifth
// gold-standard individual service page (docs/30-WEBSITE-MASTER-SPEC.md §20, §20A), same approach
// as /hoarding-cleanup-san-jose/, /estate-cleanout-san-jose/ and /deep-cleaning-san-jose/: copy
// reconciled from docs/ASEPTACLEAN-SERVICE-LANDING-PAGE-SYSTEM.md §38 under §20A.1's order —
// landing copy → map to current route → check active/gated state → check claims, pricing and
// scope → check current copy authority → then implement.
//
//   01 SERVICE HERO             photograph + overlay, left-aligned, service confirmed on sight
//   02 CLEAN FOR THE HANDOFF    the reframe: a move-out clean has a job to do, not an adjective —
//                               "ready for" sequence + what needs to be clean before keys change
//   03 EMPTY ACCESS             this page's signature movement — an empty home exposes what
//                               furniture was hiding; large photo + what becomes visible +
//                               empty-does-not-mean-clean
//   04 SCOPE + TIMING           written scope by room, what is not automatic, movers-then-cleaners
//                               sequencing — combined per §20A.6
//   05 CLEANING VS DAMAGE       cleaning/damage boundary, heavier-than-expected conditions, and the
//                               security-deposit boundary — the honest-limits movement this page
//                               needs that deep-cleaning does not (no landlord/tenant relationship
//                               there)
//   06 WHAT PROOF EXISTS + FIT + PRICE   the honest no-photo-gallery gap, fit/price logic
//   07 QUESTIONS                seven, the ones that decide the purchase
//   08 CLOSE                    "When does the property need to be ready?"
//
// THE SPECIALIST THESIS IS §20A.5's, unchanged: **get the property ready for the next handoff.**
// Every movement reinforces it without repeating the sentence — the hero states it, 02 explains
// what "ready" means for THIS property's next use, 03 is why a move-out is a real cleaning
// opportunity (access), 04 is the written scope that makes the thesis binding, 05 is the honest
// limit (cleaning is not repair, not a deposit guarantee), 06 is the honest evidence gap plus fit
// and price, 07 answers the objections a tenant, owner or agent actually has.
//
// NOT A DEEP-CLEANING CLONE WITH THE CTA CHANGED. Per the task brief's explicit instruction, this
// page is built around property TRANSITION, not buildup/detail. Concretely different from
// /deep-cleaning-san-jose/:
//   - No "look closer" room-by-room condition-recognition movement built around buildup severity.
//     This page's equivalent movement (03) is about ACCESS — what an empty home exposes — not
//     about how much grease or dust has accumulated. The photographic subject is a vacated room,
//     not a lived-in one.
//   - The scope movement here is framed around the property's NEXT USE (rental handoff, sale,
//     new tenant, family transfer) and sequenced against movers/repairs/trash removal — deep
//     cleaning has no handoff-timing content at all, because a one-time detailed clean of an
//     occupied home has no "who gets the keys next" question.
//   - This page carries a dedicated cleaning-vs-damage-vs-SECURITY-DEPOSIT movement. Deep cleaning
//     has a cleaning-vs-damage note folded into its scope section; this page needs a stronger,
//     separate version because the landlord/tenant relationship raises a claim Aseptaclean must
//     never make (deposit return, inspection passage) that simply does not arise on a one-time
//     residential deep clean.
//   - Deep cleaning's fit section reframes "routine cleaning vs detailed cleaning." This page's
//     fit section instead reframes "move-out cleaning vs property cleanout" — the basic-option
//     comparison a move-out visitor actually needs is "do I need cleaning or do I need the
//     property cleared first," not "do I need more scrubbing."
//   - Different close line, different price-driver list order (occupancy/handoff timing leads
//     here; buildup leads on deep cleaning), different image (a vacated room, not no image).
//
// DISTINCTION FROM PROPERTY CLEANOUTS (`/property-cleanouts-san-jose/`), stated explicitly on the
// page itself (movement 06's fit column) per the task brief: Property Cleanouts is for a property
// that still has contents blocking access — clearing has to happen before cleaning can start. This
// page is for a property that is already mostly accessible, where the primary remaining need is
// cleaning. The two pages are sequential, not overlapping, and each links to the other at the
// point a visitor might be in the wrong one.
//
// WHAT THIS REPLACES. The previous build: a 21-line wrapper around the shared
// `ServicePageLayout.astro` template, fed by `moveOutCleaningPage` in doc27ServicePages.ts (H1
// "Move-In & Move-Out Cleaning" — a conflated title covering two distinct intents on one page).
// 1,067 words, one H1, SIXTEEN H2s, 4 H3s, 3 <img> (all nav/footer chrome, zero content
// photography), 81 links (mostly nav/footer). §20A.3 is explicit that move-in cleaning is not yet
// a routed decision — no dedicated page, no nav entry — so this rebuild's H1 and thesis narrow to
// MOVE-OUT only, matching the route's own slug and the task brief's scope. The move-in intent
// remains servable through this route's existing "already empty" framing where it naturally
// overlaps (an empty home before occupancy is the same access condition as an empty home after
// move-out), but the page does not carry a second, equally-weighted move-in argument the way the
// old H1 implied. Approved language from the old record — the written-scope framing, the FAQ
// answer on security deposits, the "landlord/property manager" audience — is mined for copy below,
// not discarded. Full accounting in docs/05-DECISIONS-LOG.md.
//
// ROUTE STATE IS UNCHANGED AND DELIBERATELY SO. Same slug, same canonical, same `index, follow`,
// same sitemap membership (confirmed indexable in sitemap.xml.ts and AGENTS.md §2's route table).
// No route was created, renamed, redirected or re-indexed. `megaNav`'s "Move-In & Move-Out" nav
// label in src/data/site.ts is untouched — that is shared navigation data, out of scope for a
// single-page content rebuild, and changing it would affect every page that renders the header.
//
// PRICING — AGENTS.md §4 IS ABSOLUTE. The landing-page source's "$1,350" starting-price sentence
// appears three times in §38 (hero, cost section, final CTA). AGENTS.md §1.1 names this exact
// figure as barred pending an owner decision reversing §4, and doc 30 §20A.17 requires the same
// check before any publication. No current owner decision reverses §4, so no figure appears
// anywhere on this page — not in the hero, not near price logic, not in a footnote. Price logic is
// cost drivers + the $195 assessment framing only, exactly as the four prior rebuilds ship.
//
// SECURITY-DEPOSIT / INSPECTION LANGUAGE — the task brief's strongest constraint for this specific
// page. Never guaranteed anywhere on this page: full deposit return, landlord approval, passing
// inspection, no deductions, or that the home will "look brand new." Movement 05 states the
// boundary in the landing-page source's own factual register ("A detailed move-out clean can
// address the cleaning portion of the handoff. It cannot control damage-related deductions or a
// landlord's final decision.") and the FAQ repeats it as a direct answer to the question a tenant
// actually asks.
//
// NO CredentialBar ON THIS ROUTE. Same reasoning as the four prior rebuilds — both gated
// credential-bar claims (insurance wording, organic pathogen endorsement) are release-gated by
// AGENTS.md §3 pending a current COI match, and dropping a gated claim from an indexable page is
// the conservative direction. The component is untouched; only this call site omits it.
//
// NO EMBEDDED FORM. /request-assessment/ (this page's former destination, with its
// `?service=move-out` preselect into AssessmentForm.astro) was retired 2026-09-06 — owner
// decision, docs/05-CURRENT-DECISIONS.md. This page still has no form of its own, so its CTAs
// now follow the standing fallback rule and link to the Contact page's embedded form instead.
// cleaning"). No new option was added to the form.
//
// IMAGE — HERO REPOINTED 2026-09-05 to the owner image package, slot 02
// `property-interior-introduction`.
//
// It replaces `condition-development-reset-aismallard.jpg` (aismallard, Wikimedia Commons,
// CC BY-SA 3.0), whose attribution lived only in this comment and never on the rendered page —
// the licence exposure recorded in docs/06-ASSET-MANIFEST.md. Removing the placement closes it
// for this route.
//
// SUBJECT MATCH IS UNCHANGED, and it is the reason this page can carry an empty room at all. The
// core argument (movement 03) is literally "the home is empty, and that access is the
// opportunity", so an unfurnished interior IS this page's subject rather than an implied "after"
// shot: it is not paired with any "before" image, it is not captioned as a result, and the body
// copy explicitly disclaims completed-work status ("empty does not always mean clean"). Slot 02
// is the package's own introduction image — a bright unfurnished residential interior — and it
// plays the same [ATMOS]-class role the Commons file played, with a provenance the manifest can
// actually account for.
//
// Original illustrative work, NOT Aseptaclean project work, NOT a client property and NOT proof
// of a completed job or a before/after pair. `[ATMOS]`-class under
// docs/21-CLAIMS-AND-COMPLIANCE-LAW.md §6: no crew, no vehicle, no equipment, no work in progress.
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import BaseLayout from "@layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../../assets/aseptaclean/property-interior-introduction.png";
import { srcsetWidths } from "@components/imageWidths";
import { legal, site } from "@data/site";

const canonicalPath = "/move-out-cleaning-san-jose/";

// REWRITTEN from `moveOutCleaningPage.seoTitle` / `.metaDescription` (doc27ServicePages.ts), which
// described the old conflated "Move-In & Move-Out Cleaning" framing. This rebuild's H1 and thesis
// narrow to move-out only, so title/description are brought into agreement per §20A.22.
const title = "Move-Out Cleaning in San Jose | Aseptaclean";
const description =
  "Move-out cleaning in San Jose and the South Bay & Peninsula, scoped around the property's next handoff. The written scope is agreed before the crew arrives, not decided on walkthrough day.";

// §20A.8's destination rule + §20A.9's service branch. `?service=move-out` is read by
// AssessmentForm.astro (see that component's service-context block, added alongside this page).
const assessmentHref = "/contact/#contact-form";
const phoneHref = site.business.phoneUri;
const smsHref = site.business.smsUri;

// Movement 02's "ready for" sequence — the handoff-defines-scope reframe from the task brief,
// kept as short typographic fragments rather than six equal tiles.
const readyFor = [
  "a buyer walkthrough",
  "a landlord or property-manager inspection",
  "a new tenant",
  "listing photography",
  "a family handoff",
  "repair or contractor work"
] as const;

// Movement 03: what becomes visible once furniture and belongings are gone.
const exposed = [
  "dust lines along the walls",
  "baseboard buildup",
  "marks on doors and frames",
  "grease in the kitchen",
  "dirt inside cabinets",
  "shower and fixture buildup",
  "dirty window tracks",
  "dust in closets",
  "floor edges that were covered"
] as const;

// What cleaning does not automatically fix, even once it is visible — closes movement 03 before
// the page moves into the written scope, per the task brief's sequencing.
const notFixedByAccess = [
  "wall damage",
  "flooring damage",
  "scratches",
  "permanent staining",
  "paint damage",
  "worn finishes"
] as const;

// Movement 04's written scope, grouped by room — from §38's "What Can Be Included?" section,
// reworded to match this page's own voice rather than quoted verbatim.
const scope = [
  { area: "Kitchen", items: ["counters", "backsplash", "sink and faucets", "stovetop", "cabinet fronts", "appliance exteriors", "baseboards", "doors and frames", "floors"] },
  { area: "Bathrooms", items: ["shower and tub", "shower glass", "toilet", "sink and faucets", "mirrors", "vanity", "baseboards", "doors and frames", "floors"] },
  { area: "Bedrooms & living areas", items: ["reachable surfaces", "closets and shelves", "baseboards", "doors and frames", "trim", "switch plates", "floor edges", "floors"] }
] as const;

const scopeExtras = [
  "Inside empty cabinets and drawers",
  "Interior windows, sills and tracks",
  "Inside the oven",
  "Inside the refrigerator",
  "Garage areas",
  "Other detail work agreed on before the job"
] as const;

const notAutomatic = [
  "Inside every cabinet or drawer",
  "Large amounts of interior glass",
  "Heavy oven or refrigerator buildup",
  "Garage detailing",
  "Unusually heavy buildup",
  "Areas still blocked by belongings"
] as const;

// Movement 04's timing/sequencing content — the task brief's "work backward from the handoff."
const sequence = [
  "Belongings, boxes and trash are out",
  "Repairs, painting or flooring work are finished",
  "Aseptaclean cleans the agreed scope",
  "Final review against the written scope",
  "Handoff to the next step"
] as const;

// Movement 05: what cleaning can address vs. what it cannot fix — the security-deposit boundary
// is kept as its own dedicated statement per the task brief, not folded into this list.
const canHelp = ["dirt", "dust", "grease", "soap buildup", "food residue", "normal surface soil"] as const;
const cannotFix = ["holes", "broken items", "scratches", "chipped surfaces", "burn marks", "damaged paint", "etched glass", "damaged stone", "worn finishes", "broken flooring"] as const;

// What a heavier-than-expected condition can look like — the task brief's "if the condition
// changes the job, we tell you" rule, kept short and factual, not fear-based.
const heavierConditions = [
  "heavy grease or buildup",
  "excessive pet hair",
  "significant debris",
  "areas that are not reachable yet"
] as const;

const goodFit = [
  "the property is empty or nearly empty",
  "you want a detailed clean, not a quick wipe-down",
  "you want the scope written before work starts",
  "cabinets, appliances or windows need attention",
  "there is a real handoff date to work backward from",
  "you do not want to argue later about what was included"
] as const;

const poorFit = [
  "you only want the lowest-cost wipe-down",
  "you need recurring housekeeping, not a one-time clean",
  "the property still holds too much to reach the surfaces",
  "the main need is repairs, not cleaning",
  "you expect cleaning to guarantee a deposit or inspection result"
] as const;

const priceDrivers = [
  "square footage and bedroom/bathroom count",
  "whether the property is empty or still occupied",
  "current condition and amount of buildup",
  "cabinets, appliances and windows included",
  "garage or other extra areas",
  "how much detail work you want",
  "your handoff date"
] as const;

// §20A.18: 4–7 highest-value service-specific questions. The security-deposit question leads
// because it is the single highest-stakes claim this page must get right.
const faq = [
  [
    "Will this guarantee my security deposit back?",
    [
      "No. No cleaning company can honestly promise that.",
      "A detailed move-out clean can address the cleaning portion of the handoff. It cannot control damage-related deductions, unpaid rent, normal wear, or a landlord's final decision."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "Should the home be empty before you clean?",
    [
      "For a true move-out clean, yes. Furniture, boxes, trash and food should be out so the crew is cleaning, not packing or sorting your belongings.",
      "If items will still be there, tell us before the job — it can change the scope and the price."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "When should I schedule the clean relative to the movers?",
    [
      "After. If repairs, painting or flooring work are still happening, the property can get dirty again before the handoff.",
      "The better order is: move out, finish other work, remove trash, then clean."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "Do you clean inside cabinets, the oven, or the refrigerator?",
    ["Yes, when they are empty and included in the scope. Heavy buildup can take more time and is priced before the job rather than assumed."]
  ],
  [
    "Do you clean windows and garages?",
    ["Interior windows and garage areas can be added to the scope. Tell us during the estimate if you want them included."]
  ],
  [
    "What if the property is dirtier than expected?",
    [
      "We tell you. If the condition changes the scope — heavier buildup, pet hair, debris, or areas we cannot yet reach — we say so before doing the extra work, not after.",
      "We do not quietly expand the job and surprise you with a bigger bill."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "Do you have a landlord or property checklist I should send?",
    ["Yes, send it. A move-out inspection list or property checklist helps us compare what you want cleaned against what the landlord or manager is actually looking for."]
  ]
] as const;

const schema = [
  {
    "@type": "Service",
    name: "Move-Out Cleaning in San Jose",
    serviceType: "Move-out cleaning",
    provider: { "@id": new URL("/#business", site.urls.site).href },
    areaServed: { "@type": "City", name: "San Jose" }
  },
  {
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: site.urls.home },
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: 2,
        name: "Detailed Cleaning",
        item: new URL("/detailed-cleaning/", site.urls.site).href
      },
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: 3,
        name: "Move-Out Cleaning",
        item: new URL(canonicalPath, site.urls.site).href
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: faq.map(([question, answer]) => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      name: question,
      acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: answer.join("\n\n") }
    }))
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath={canonicalPath} schema={schema}>
  {/* ══ 01 · SERVICE HERO ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      §20A.7: location context → unmistakable service H1 → specialist thesis → service CTA →
      phone as secondary → service-relevant image. The empty-room photograph is this page's own
      subject matter — see the top-of-file note on why this is the first route to use it. */}
  <section class="mo-hero" aria-labelledby="mo-title">
    <div class="mo-hero__media">
      <Image
        src={heroImage}
        alt=""
        layout="full-width"
        widths={srcsetWidths(heroImage, [480, 768, 1024, 1440, 1920])}
        sizes="100vw"
        format="webp"
        quality={74}
        loading="eager"
        decoding="async"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>

    <div class="mo-hero__overlay" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <div class="mo-hero__shell">
      <div class="mo-hero__content">
        <p class="mo-hero__eyebrow">
          Move-Out Cleaning · San Jose · {site.location.serviceArea}
        </p>
        <h1 id="mo-title" class="ac-type-h1-hub">Move-Out Cleaning</h1>
        <p class="mo-hero__lede">
          When the belongings are leaving, the property gets one of its best chances for a true
          top-to-bottom cleaning. We define the work around what it needs before the handoff.
        </p>
        <p class="mo-hero__thesis">Get the property ready for whoever gets it next.</p>

        <div class="mo-hero__actions">
          <a class="mo-btn mo-btn--light" href={assessmentHref}>Get My Move-Out Estimate</a>
          {phoneHref && (
            <a class="mo-hero__call" href={phoneHref}>Call {site.business.phone}</a>
          )}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 02 · CLEAN FOR THE HANDOFF ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      §20A.6's reframe movement. The core argument: the goal is not "clean," it is "ready for the
      next person" — and what "ready" means depends on what the property is being handed to. */}
  <section class="mo-handoff" aria-labelledby="handoff-title">
    <div class="mo-handoff__inner">
      <p class="mo-label">What the clean is for</p>
      <h2 id="handoff-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">A move-out clean has a job to do.</h2>
      <p class="mo-lede">
        The property may need to be ready for a buyer walkthrough, a landlord inspection, a new
        tenant, or a family handoff. A quick surface clean may be enough for one property. Another
        may need a lot more detail. The end goal is what decides which.
      </p>

      <div class="mo-ready">
        <p class="mo-ready__label">Ready for</p>
        <ul class="mo-ready__list">
          {readyFor.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p class="mo-statement">The handoff defines the scope.</p>
      <p class="mo-handoff__question">What needs to be clean before the keys change hands?</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 03 · EMPTY ACCESS CHANGES THE JOB ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
      This page's signature movement, per the task brief. Large photo (the hero already carries
      it, so this movement leads with the list) + what becomes visible + empty-does-not-mean-clean
      + what access does not fix. */}
  <section class="mo-access" aria-labelledby="access-title">
    <div class="mo-access__inner">
      <div class="mo-access__lead">
        <p class="mo-label">Why this is a real opportunity</p>
        <h2 id="access-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">
          An empty home gives us access you normally do not have.
        </h2>
        <p class="mo-lede">
          Furniture, rugs, boxes and decor can hide a lot. Once they are gone, previously blocked
          areas become reachable — wall edges, baseboards, closet floors, cabinet interiors, and
          flooring that has been covered for years.
        </p>
        <p class="mo-statement">Empty does not always mean clean.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="mo-exposed">
        <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">What often shows up once furniture is gone</h3>
        <ul class="mo-inline-list">
          {exposed.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
        </ul>

        <p class="mo-access__note">
          Access does not automatically fix what it reveals. Cleaning does not repair:
        </p>
        <ul class="mo-inline-list">
          {notFixedByAccess.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 04 · SCOPE + TIMING ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      §20A.6 combines the written scope with movers/access sequencing — both answer "what happens
      and in what order," so they belong in one movement rather than two. §20A.13: scope and
      exclusions are conversion content. */}
  <section class="mo-scope" aria-labelledby="scope-title">
    <div class="mo-scope__inner">
      <p class="mo-label">The written scope</p>
      <h2 id="scope-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">We define the scope before the job.</h2>
      <p class="mo-lede">
        “Move-out cleaning” means different things to different companies. You think the oven is
        included; the cleaner does not. You expect the window tracks; they were never part of the
        quote. We avoid that by writing the scope down before the crew arrives.
      </p>

      <div class="mo-scope__grid">
        {scope.map((group) => (
          <div class="mo-scope__col">
            <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">{group.area}</h3>
            <ul class="mo-rule-list mo-rule-list--tight">
              {group.items.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
            </ul>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="mo-scope__extras">
        <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">Other work, when added to the scope</h3>
        <ul class="mo-inline-list">
          {scopeExtras.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="mo-notauto">
        <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">What is not automatically included</h3>
        <ul class="mo-minus-list">
          {notAutomatic.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p class="mo-statement mo-statement--wide">
          If it adds meaningful work, it goes in the scope. We would rather price it first than
          surprise you on the day.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mo-sequence">
        <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">Cleaning works best after the movers are done.</h3>
        <p>
          If repairs, painting or flooring work are still happening, the property can get dirty
          again before the handoff. Work backward from the date it actually needs to be ready:
        </p>
        <ol class="mo-sequence__list">
          {sequence.map((step) => <li>{step}</li>)}
        </ol>
        <p>
          If movers and cleaners have to overlap, tell us — the scope may need to be split or
          adjusted rather than done in one pass.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="mo-disclaimer">{legal.scopeDisclaimer}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 05 · CLEANING VS DAMAGE + SECURITY DEPOSIT ═══════════════════════════════════════════
      The task brief's most important boundary movement. Kept separate from the deep-cleaning
      page's version because this page needs an explicit, standalone security-deposit statement a
      one-time residential detailed clean never has to make. */}
  <section class="mo-boundary" aria-labelledby="boundary-title">
    <div class="mo-boundary__inner">
      <p class="mo-label">What cleaning can and cannot do</p>
      <h2 id="boundary-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">Cleaning cannot repair damage.</h2>

      <div class="mo-boundary__pair">
        <div class="mo-note">
          <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">Cleaning can help with</h3>
          <ul class="mo-inline-list">
            {canHelp.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="mo-note">
          <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">Cleaning does not fix</h3>
          <ul class="mo-inline-list">
            {cannotFix.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="mo-statement mo-statement--wide">
        If something is damaged, more scrubbing does not turn it into cleaning. We will tell you
        when we see the difference.
      </p>

      <div class="mo-deposit">
        <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-feature">We will not promise your security deposit.</h3>
        <p>
          No cleaning company can control a lease, unpaid rent, normal wear, pre-existing damage,
          or a landlord's inspection standards. A detailed move-out clean can address the cleaning
          portion of the handoff. It cannot control damage-related deductions or a landlord's final
          decision.
        </p>
        <p>
